<h1 id="s1"><span class="n">1</span>Introduction</h1>
<h2 id="s1-1"><span class="n">1.1</span>What a test is here</h2>
<p>Every test boots the real chip. The testbench loads a firmware image into ROM, releases reset, the chip powers itself up, and the Ibex CPU runs the image. A test then has up to two halves:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>A firmware half</b> — a C file that runs on the CPU, programs registers, and reports PASSED or FAILED.</li>
<li><b>A testbench half</b> — a UVM test class that drives or watches pins, clocks or the bus, and adds its own checks.</li>
</ul>
<p>A test can use either half or both. Independently of your code, every test also checks every bus transaction in the chip: 56 AXI monitors and the fabric scoreboard run all the time.</p>
<h2 id="s1-2"><span class="n">1.2</span>Prerequisites</h2>
<ul>
<li>C for embedded systems (volatile, register access, interrupts)</li>
<li>SystemVerilog and the basics of UVM (tests, sequences, <code>uvm_info</code>)</li>
<li>Access to the simulation server with Cadence Xcelium</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="s1-3"><span class="n">1.3</span>Conventions</h2>
<p><code>my_test</code> stands for the name you choose. Paths are relative to the repository root. Shell commands are bash; csh users replace <code>env.sh</code> by <code>env.csh</code>.</p>

<!-- ================================================================== 2 -->
<h1 id="s2"><span class="n">2</span>Before you start</h1>
<h2 id="s2-1"><span class="n">2.1</span>One-time setup</h2>
<pre><code>git clone --recurse-submodules git@github.com:auxeraglobaltech/amba-titan.git
cd amba-titan
~/.local/opt/python-3.11.15-titan/bin/python3 -m venv .venv      <span class="c"># project Python</span>
.venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt
git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/accellera-official/uvm-core .tools/uvm-core   <span class="c"># for the lint check (optional)</span></code></pre>
<h2 id="s2-2"><span class="n">2.2</span>Every shell</h2>
<pre><code><span class="c"># load your Cadence setup first (xrun on PATH), then:</span>
source uvm/sim/env.sh            <span class="c"># RISC-V gcc, project Python; prints what it found</span></code></pre>
<h2 id="s2-3"><span class="n">2.3</span>Check the environment works</h2>
<pre><code>uvm/sim/run.sh fw_hello          <span class="c"># about 1 minute; expect:  RESULT: PASSED  fw_hello</span></code></pre>
<div class="caution"><b class="h">CAUTION</b>Run one simulation at a time — the server and the build directory are shared. Before you change anything, run the regression (<code>uvm/sim/regress.sh</code>) so you know it was green when you started.</div>
<h2 id="s2-4"><span class="n">2.4</span>Where files go</h2>
<figure>@@FIG:ug_files@@
<figcaption><b>Figure 2-1.</b> The files you add for a test (green), the ones you extend only when a test needs something new (brown), and the ones you never edit (grey).</figcaption></figure>

